When a table’s own height is auto, Ladybird sizes a cell that has a
percentage height as if its height were auto: The percentage takes no
part in the row measures or in the second pass of row sizing, and the
cell is exactly as tall as its row. That matches all three engines.
Blink’s ComputeMinimumRowBlockSize() turns a cell’s percentage into its
row’s percent constraint, which matters only when the table has extra
height to distribute, and TableRowLayoutAlgorithm::Layout() gives the
cell its row’s height. WebKit’s logicalHeightForRowSizing() evaluates
the percentage against zero while sizing the rows of an auto-height
table, and layoutRows() stretches each cell to its row through the
cell’s computeIntrinsicPadding(). And in Gecko, CalcCellActualBSize()
records the percentage on the row through SetPctBSize(), GetBSizeBasis()
returns zero for an auto-height table, and nsTableRowFrame::DidResize()
sets each cell to its row’s height.
